      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this markdown fragment carefully.\n\nThe document is an OIML Certificate of Conformity for a water meter type MA408, issued by Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM).\n\nKey details:\n- Certificate number: R49/2024-A-SK1-26.01\n- Scheme: A\n- Member state: Slovakia\n- Date issued: 10 September 2026\n- Issuing authority: Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM), Geologická 9966/1, 821 06 Bratislava-Podunajské Biskupice, Slovakia\n- Person responsible: Ing. Dušan Šmigura, PhD., Director of PCB\n- Applicant: Shenzhen Kaifa Technology (Chengdu) Co., Ltd., No. 99 Tianquan Road, Hi-Tech Development Zone, Chengdu, Sichuan, P.R. China\n- Manufacturer: same\n- Certified type: MA408 ultrasonic water meter\n- Recommendation: OIML R 49, Edition 2024\n- Accuracy class: 2\n\nTest reports:\n- Type Evaluation Report: 2026/ER040/SK1, 10 September 2026, 29 pages, role: type_evaluation_report\n- Technical Documentation File: Shenzhen Kaifa_MA408_00_OIML R49:2024, 9 September 2026, 118 pages, role: documentation_file\n\nRevision history:\n- Revision 0, 10 September 2026, Certificate first issued\n\nNow the technical data — Page 4 has a table with MA408 (DN15), MA408 (DN20), MA408 (DN25) — this is a model matrix!\n\n| Characteristics | Unit | MA408 (DN15) | MA408 (DN20) | MA408 (DN25) |\n| Nominal diameter DN | mm | DN15 | DN20 | DN25 |\n| Permanent flowrate Q₃ | m³/h | 2,5 | 4 | 6,3 |\n| Minimum flowrate Q₁ | m³/h | 0.015625 / 0.01 / 0.00625 / 0.005 | 0.025 / 0.016 / 0.01 / 0.008 | 0.039375 / 0.0252 / 0.01575 / 0.0126 |\n| Transitional flowrate Q₂ | m³/h | 0.025 / 0.016 / 0.01 / 0.008 | 0.04 / 0.0256 / 0.016 / 0.0128 | 0.063 / 0.04032 / 0.0252 / 0.02016 |\n| Overload flowrate Q₄ | m³/h | 3,125 | 5 | 7,875 |\n| Ratio Q₃/Q₁ | R | R160, R250, R400, R500 (all sizes) |\n| Ratio Q₂/Q₁ | – | 1,6 |\n| Connection thread | mm | G ¾ B | G1 B | G1 ¼ B |\n| Construction length L | mm | 110/115/145/165 | 130/165/190/195/220 | 175/200/225/260 |\n| Installation orientation | – | H/V |\n| Water temperature range | °C | 0,1 to 50 (T50) |\n\nSo the Q₁ and Q₂ values are slash-separated lists — these correspond to the different R ratios (R160, R250, R400, R500). Let me verify:\n\nFor DN15: Q₃ = 2.5 m³/h\n- R160: Q₁ = 2.5/160 = 0.015625 ✓\n- R250: Q₁ = 2.5/250 = 0.01 ✓\n- R400: Q₁ = 2.5/400 = 0.00625 ✓\n- R500: Q₁ = 2.5/500 = 0.005 ✓\n\nSo the slash-separated values correspond to R160/R250/R400/R500 ratios.\n\nQ₂ for DN15:\n- R160: Q₂ = 1.6 × 0.015625 = 0.025 ✓\n- R250: Q₂ = 1.6 × 0.01 = 0.016 ✓\n- R400: Q₂ = 1.6 × 0.00625 = 0.01 ✓\n- R500: Q₂ = 1.6 × 0.005 = 0.008 ✓\n\nFor DN20: Q₃ = 4\n- R160: Q₁ = 4/160 = 0.025 ✓\n- R250: Q₁ = 4/250 = 0.016 ✓\n- R400: Q₁ = 4/400 = 0.01 ✓\n- R500: Q₁ = 4/500 = 0.008 ✓\n\nQ₂ DN20:\n- R160: 1.6 × 0.025 = 0.04 ✓\n- R250: 1.6 × 0.016 = 0.0256 ✓\n- R400: 1.6 × 0.01 = 0.016 ✓\n- R500: 1.6 × 0.008 = 0.0128 ✓\n\nFor DN25: Q₃ = 6.3\n- R160: Q₁ = 6.3/160 = 0.039375 ✓\n- R250: Q₁ = 6.3/250 = 0.0252 ✓\n- R400: Q₁ = 6.3/400 = 0.01575 ✓\n- R500: Q₁ = 6.3/500 = 0.0126 ✓\n\nQ₂ DN25:\n- R160: 0.063 ✓\n- R250: 0.04032 ✓\n- R400: 0.0252 ✓\n- R500: 0.02016 ✓\n\nSo the family structure is: 3 DN sizes × 4 R ratios = 12 model variants. But the certificate organizes primarily by DN size. Hmm, how to model this?\n\nOption 1: models are the DN sizes (MA408 DN15, DN20, DN25), and the R-ratio variation is a configuration axis. The Q₁/Q₂ values vary by both DN and R.\n\nOption 2: models are the 12 combinations.\n\nI think a reasonable approach: model_family.models with model_id \"MA408 DN15\", \"MA408 DN20\", \"MA408 DN25\". Then:\n\n- model_level for: nominal_diameter, q3, q4, connection_thread, construction_length — these vary by DN.\n- The R ratio is a choice within each size: ratio_q3_q1 = R160/R250/R400/R500 for all sizes.\n- q1 and q2 vary by both DN and R ratio — this is 2-dimensional. This could go in config_level with axis being both DN and ratio... Actually config_level has {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value}]}. The condition could be \"DN15 + R160\" etc. Or I could use matrix_tables.\n\nActually, the cleanest: matrix_tables for the whole technical data table, plus model_level for the DN-varying attributes, and config_level for q1/q2 with axis \"meter ratio (R) within nominal diameter\" and conditions like \"MA408 DN15, R160\".\n\nAlternatively, make 12 models: MA408-DN15-R160, etc. That's more granular but makes the matrix complete. Hmm. The R example in the prompt shows models defined by the varying attribute. Here the primary designation table columns are DN sizes. I'll go with 3 models by DN, and treat R ratio as a configuration axis.\n\nLet me structure:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"MA408\" (the type covers a family of variants)\n- models: [{model_id: \"MA408 DN15\", label: \"MA408 (DN15)\"}, ...]\n\nActually let me think about the model_id naming. The table header says \"MA408 (DN15)\", \"MA408 (DN20)\", \"MA408 (DN25)\". I'll use those as model_id.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level (whole-cert, apply to all):\n- water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C, with note \"(T50)\" → temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- installation_orientation: \"H/V\" (dimensionless)\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6\n- ratio_q3_q1: hmm, this is \"R160, R250, R400, R500 (all sizes)\" — this is a list of possible values across all sizes. It's the same for all models. Since it's a set of alternatives (not per-model variation), could keep as type_level with value as string \"R160, R250, R400, R500\"? Or as config_level axis? I think it's better as a type_level with value list... but rule says lists of values that are model matrices should be model_level. Here it's not per-model — it applies to all sizes. It's a set of selectable ratios. Hmm.\n\nActually, I could model the R ratio as a config axis, and Q1/Q2 as config_level entries. Let me think again.\n\nconfig_level structure:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_flowrate_q1\",\n  \"axis\": \"nominal diameter × meter ratio R\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"MA408 (DN15), R160\", \"value\": 0.015625, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThat's 12 conditions per attribute. That's verbose but correct.\n\nAlternatively, matrix_tables: I could put the full table as a matrix table with rows per DN and columns including the lists. And then expand.\n\nLet me design the output:\n\nmatrix_tables:\n1. name: \"Technical and metrological data\"\n   columns: [\"characteristic\", \"unit\", \"ma408_dn15\", \"ma408_dn20\", \"ma408_dn25\"]\n   rows: one per characteristic with raw values.\n   \nActually the schema example shows rows as objects with column values. The table here is transposed (characteristics in rows, models in columns). I could either keep it as-is (rows = characteristics) or transpose (rows = models). The R137 example has rows = models. For consistency and usefulness, I might transpose: rows = models.\n\nBut then the Q1/Q2 lists are multi-valued within a cell. Hmm.\n\nLet me do this:\n\nmatrix_tables: [\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Technical and metrological data by nominal diameter\",\n    \"columns\": [\"model\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"q3\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q4\", \"connection_thread\", \"construction_length\"],\n    \"column_units\": [null, \"mm\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"mm\", \"mm\"],\n    \"rows\": [\n      {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN15)\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"DN15\", \"q3\": 2.5, \"q1\": \"0.015625 / 0.01 / 0.00625 / 0.005\", ...},\n      ...\n    ],\n    \"description\": \"Q1 and Q2 values listed in order of meter ratio R = R160, R250, R400, R500; construction length options correspond to the different R ratios... \"\n  }\n]\n\nWait — construction length: DN15: 110/115/145/165. That's 4 values matching 4 R ratios! DN20: 130/165/190/195/220 — that's 5 values, doesn't match 4. DN25: 175/200/225/260 — 4 values. Hmm, DN20 has 5 lengths. So the lengths don't necessarily map 1:1 to R ratios. So construction length is just a list of available lengths per DN.\n\nOK. Given complexity, I'll provide:\n- model_level entries for attributes that vary by DN: nominal_diameter, permanent_flowrate_q3, overload_flowrate_q4, connection_thread, construction_length (list of options — hmm, value should be number or range; a list of 4-5 discrete lengths... The rule says lists of values → model matrix, but here it's multiple length options within one model. I could record as string \"110/115/145/165\"? Or config axis \"construction length option\"? I think a string list is acceptable given rule 8 (don't fabricate structure). Actually rule 2 says value can be a string. Let me record construction_length as a string of options with note. Alternatively config_level with axis \"construction length option\". Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe cleaner to record q1 and q2 with config_level axis \"meter ratio R\", with conditions like \"MA408 (DN15), R160\". That gives explicit pairing. 12 entries each. It's verbose but precise and follows the schema.\n\nAnd ratio_q3_q1 as type_level? It's \"R160, R250, R400, R500 (all sizes)\" — same for all models. It's the set of available ratios. I'll record it as a config axis attribute? Hmm, config_level values are {condition, value}. For ratio, I could do:\n\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"ratio_q3_q1\",\n  \"axis\": \"meter ratio option\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"all models\", \"value\": \"R160\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"all models\", \"value\": \"R250\"},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n\nThat's awkward. Maybe simpler: type_level with value \"R160 / R250 / R400 / R500\" as string, unit \"R\"? Hmm, unit R... The unit column says \"R\". Value is a string list. I think a string is fine: {\"value\": [\"R160\",\"R250\",\"R400\",\"R500\"]}? The schema value shape allows number | {min,max} | string. Lists aren't in value shape. I'll use a string \"R160, R250, R400, R500\". Hmm, or maybe I should treat ratio as the config axis and note it in the q1/q2 config entries.\n\nLet me decide: I'll use config_level for q1 and q2 with axis \"nominal diameter and meter ratio R\". Conditions: \"MA408 (DN15), R160\" etc. And type_level ratio_q3_q1 as string \"R160, R250, R400, R500 (available ratios for all sizes)\"... no, keep clean: value \"R160/R250/R400/R500\", unit null? unit \"R\"? Hmm — \"Ratio Q₃/Q₁ | R\" — unit column \"R\" means the ratio is expressed as R-value. I'd say unit_symbol: \"R\" is odd; ratio is dimensionless. I'll put unit_symbol null and value string listing options. Actually wait — better: since the ratio is a selectable configuration that also determines Q1/Q2, and I'm encoding ratio into the config conditions, maybe I don't need ratio_q3_q1 in type_level at all. But rule 8 says don't fabricate, not don't include. I'll include it as type_level string for completeness.\n\nNow Page 5 technical specifications (apply to whole type / all models):\n\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_map: 16 bar\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp 40\" with pressure loss 0.40 bar? The row: \"Pressure loss class Δp | bar / – | 0,40 / Δp 40\". So two things: max pressure loss 0.40 bar and class Δp40. I'll do:\n  - maximum_pressure_loss: {value: 0.4, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n  - pressure_loss_class: {value: \"Δp40\"} (dimensionless class)\n- mpe_upper_flowrates: ±2% at ϑ ≤ 30 °C, ±3% at ϑ > 30 °C — this is config_level? axis \"water temperature\". Values: {condition: \"Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4 and ϑ ≤ 30 °C\", value: 2, unit: \"%\"}... hmm the ± sign. Value ±2 → I could record as 2 with note, or \"±2\". Rule says numbers not strings. ±2 means tolerance ±2%. I'll record value: 2, unit \"%\", and attribute name \"maximum permissible error upper flow zones\" — maybe include condition mention of ±? Hmm. Actually MPE ±2% — the value magnitude is 2. I'll record value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\", condition \"upper flow zones (Q₂ ≤ Q ≤ Q₄), water temperature ≤ 30 °C\". And second: value 3, condition \"upper flow zones (Q₂ ≤ Q ≤ Q₄), water temperature > 30 °C\". These are config_level with axis \"flow zone / water temperature\".\n- mpe_lower_flowrates: ±5% → config_level or type_level: {value: 5, unit: \"%\"} condition lower flow zones. Since MPE overall varies by config, I could make one attribute \"maximum_permissible_error\" with axis \"flow zone and water temperature\", values:\n  - {condition: \"lower flow rates (Q1 ≤ Q < Q2)\", value: 5}\n  - {condition: \"upper flow rates (Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4), water temperature ≤ 30 °C\", value: 2}\n  - {condition: \"upper flow rates (Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4), water temperature > 30 °C\", value: 3}\n  \n  That's clean. unit \"%\".\n\n- capacity_of_calculator: normal mode 999999.999 m³, testing mode 999.999999 m³ — config_level axis \"display mode\". Decimal fix: 999999,999 → 999999.999; 999,999999 → 999.999999.\n- scale_interval normal resolution: 0.001 m³\n- scale_interval high resolution: 0.000001 m³ — config_level axis \"resolution mode\"? Or two separate attributes. I'll do config_level: attribute scale_interval, axis \"resolution mode\", values [{condition: \"normal\", value: 0.001}, {condition: \"high\", value: 0.000001}], unit m³.\n- accuracy_class: 2 (also in certificate recommendation accuracy_classes)\n- mechanical_environmental_class: \"M1\"\n- climatic_class: −25 to +55 °C → range {min: -25, max: 55} °C. Note \"climatic class\" value is the range.\n- electromagnetic_environmental_class: \"E1/E2\"\n- climatic_mechanical_environmental_class_r49_1: \"B/O\" — value \"B/O\" (climatic B, mechanical O).\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- battery: non-replaceable li-battery, Umax = 3.7 V, lifetime 18 years. → characteristics:\n  - battery_type: \"non-replaceable lithium battery\"\n  - battery_maximum_voltage: 3.7 V (\"Umax = 3,7 V\" → 3.7)\n  - battery_lifetime: 18 years (unit \"a\" or \"years\"). unit_symbol: \"years\"? Use \"a\"? I'll use \"years\" — hmm canonical Unicode... The unit for year is \"a\". I'll use unit_symbol \"years\" to be safe and unit_id null. Actually maybe \"a\" is the SI symbol. I'll put \"years\".\n\nType-level also from page 3/4:\n- display_digits: 9 digits, range 999999.999 m³ (calculator capacity covers this)\n- battery lifetime 18 years also mentioned on page 3.\n\nComponents (from page 3 description): flow sensor, calculator and indication device. Non-essential parts: strainer, optical communication port, communication battery. Should I include components[]? The rule says include only if cert names sub-assemblies. The description names essential parts: flow sensor; calculator and indication device. I'll include:\n\ncomponents: [\n  {role: \"flow sensor (essential part)\", type_designations: [], ...} — hmm type_designations should be type/model identifiers. The description doesn't give separate type designations for components. But it describes them. I can put characteristics/description. The schema allows components with role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I could include description within characteristics as a free-form? characteristics{} — \"sub-characteristics\". Maybe I include \"description\" key? The schema shows components[]: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I'll include characteristics with structured values where possible:\n  - flow sensor: ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time principle; brass body; two ultrasonic transducers.\n  - calculator and indication device: LCD 9 digits; battery lithium non-replaceable.\n  \nHmm, maybe simpler to include components with role and a \"description\" field inside characteristics as {\"description\": {...}}? That's clunky. I'll include components with role, type_designations: [] maybe omitted... The schema lists type_designations[] as part of components; if none named, maybe use the description. I think including components adds value. Let me include:\n\ncomponents: [\n  {\n    \"role\": \"measuring transducer (flow sensor)\",\n    \"description\": \"Brass cylindrical body with inlet/outlet, inner plastic measuring supports, two reflection sheets in pipe centre, two ultrasonic transducers (upstream and downstream); ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time principle.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"role\": \"calculator and indication device (electronic indication device)\",\n    \"description\": \"Plastic housing mounted directly on the flow sensor; one combined PCB; electronic scrolling LCD with 9 digits (range 999999.999 m³); one non-replaceable lithium battery (lifetime 18 years).\"\n  }\n]\n\nBut schema says components[]: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. Adding a description field — is it allowed? The schema is a guide; rule 8 says don't fabricate fields not present... description is present in markdown. I'll put descriptions inside characteristics as {\"description\": \"...\"}? characteristics{} is free-form object. I'll do:\n\ncomponents: [\n  {\n    \"role\": \"flow sensor\",\n    \"essential\": true? — not in schema. Skip.\n    \"type_designations\": [],\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"description\": \"brass cylindrical body ...\",\n      \"measurement_principle\": {\"value\": \"ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time\"},\n      \"number_of_ultrasonic_transducers\": {\"value\": 2}\n    }\n  },\n  ...\n]\n\nHmm, characteristics values should be structured {value, unit_symbol, unit_id}? For consistency I'll use the structured shape.\n\nAlso non-essential parts: strainer, optical communication port, one communication battery. Could add as components with role noting non-essential:\n  {role: \"strainer (non-essential part)\", type_designations: []}\n  {role: \"optical communication port (non-essential part)\"}\n  {role: \"communication battery (non-essential part)\"}\n\nMaybe keep it lighter: include essential components with descriptions, and mention non-essential parts in certified_type.description.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (the category list includes \"Gas meter\", \"Water meter\" fits; the list gives examples with \"...\" so Water meter is fine)\n- type_designations: [\"MA408\"]\n- module_designation: \"Ultrasonic water meters with electronic indication device\"? Hmm, module designation in OIML-CS refers to module like \"Analog load cell\" etc. The page 1 overview says \"Module Designation: Ultrasonic water meters with electronic indication device\". So set module_designation to that string. Hmm, but that's more like instrument category. The overview table explicitly labels it Module Designation, so I'll use it.\n- description: compile from page 3: measures, memorizes, displays volume of water passing through measurement transducers at metering conditions; clean water residential; compact ultrasonic meter with electronic indication device; ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time principle; horizontal and vertical installation; not designed to measure reverse flow. Non-essential parts: strainer, optical communication port, one communication battery.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2024-A-SK1-26.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: hmm — not explicitly given. The certificate number contains SK1-26.01. No separate project number. Omit.\n- page_total: 7 (pages 1-7 mentioned: \"Page 2 of 7\", footer \"Page 4 of 7 pages\"). Include 7.\n- member_state: \"Slovakia\"\n- date_issued: \"10 September 2026\" → format? Probably ISO \"2026-09-10\". I'll use ISO.\n- oiml_issuer_id: not given. Omit.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\"\n- address_lines: [\"Geologická 9966/1\", \"821 06 Bratislava-Podunajské Biskupice\", \"Slovakia\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Ing. Dušan Šmigura, PhD.\" — page 1 says \"Ing. Dušan Šmigura, PhD., Director of PCB\"; page 2 signature \"Dušan Smigura\". person_title: \"Director of PCB\"\n- phone/email/website: not present, omit.\n- oiml_issuer_id: omit.\n\napplicants: [{name, address_lines}]\nmanufacturers: [{name, address_lines}]\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\"\n- edition: 2024\n- amendment: not present, omit? The number \"R49/2024-A-SK1-26.01\" — 2024 is edition. amendment null/omit.\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [\"2\"]? The class 2 for water meters. accuracy_classes: [2]? Schema says accuracy_classes[] — for R60 example it's like [\"C\", \"D\"]... probably strings. I'll use [\"2\"]. Hmm, numbers or strings? I'll use strings [\"2\"] to be safe? Actually numbers might be fine. The schema example doesn't show. I'll use [\"2\"].\n\ntest_reports:\n- {id: \"2026/ER040/SK1\", date: \"2026-09-10\", pages: 29, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"Shenzhen Kaifa_MA408_00_OIML R49:2024\", date: \"2026-09-09\", pages: 118, role: \"documentation_file\"}\n\nrevision_history:\n- {revision: \"0\", date: \"2026-09-10\", changes: \"Certificate first issued\"}\n\nfootnotes: none present.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"MA408\"\n- models: [\n    {model_id: \"MA408 (DN15)\", label: \"MA408 (DN15)\", attributes: {nominal_diameter: \"DN15\"}},\n    {model_id: \"MA408 (DN20)\", ...},\n    {model_id: \"MA408 (DN25)\", ...}\n  ]\n\nHmm — or should models include R-ratio variants? The certificate table gives Q1/Q2 as lists per ratio. I'll keep 3 models and encode ratio variation in config_level.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level:\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- installation_orientation: {value: \"H/V\"}\n- ratio_q2_q1: {value: 1.6}\n- ratio_q3_q1: {value: \"R160, R250, R400, R500\", ...} hmm. Let me make it a string \"R160 / R250 / R400 / R500\" with a note? No notes field in structured value besides footnote_markers. I'll just do {\"value\": [\"R160\", \"R250\", \"R400\", \"R500\"]}? Value shape is number | {min,max} | string. Array not allowed. I'll use string \"R160/R250/R400/R500\".\n\nHmm, wait — maybe better to treat ratio as config axis and put ratio_q3_q1 in config_level:\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"ratio_q3_q1\",\n  \"axis\": \"available meter ratio\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"all models\", \"value\": \"R160\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"all models\", \"value\": \"R250\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"all models\", \"value\": \"R400\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"all models\", \"value\": \"R500\"}\n  ]\n}\nThat's more structured. And then q1/q2 config keyed on DN + ratio.\n\nAlso type_level:\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_map: {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n- accuracy_class: {value: 2}\n- mechanical_environmental_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n- climatic_class: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- electromagnetic_environmental_class: {value: \"E1/E2\"}\n- environmental_class_r49_1: {value: \"B/O\"} — label: \"climatic_mechanical_environmental_class\" with note it's per OIML R 49-1... I'll use \"climatic_mechanical_environmental_class\" and maybe mention in matrix? Fine.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0D0\"}\n- pressure_loss_class: {value: \"Δp40\"} — from \"Δp 40\". \n- maximum_pressure_loss: {value: 0.4, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n- battery_type: {value: \"non-replaceable lithium battery\"}\n- battery_voltage_max: {value: 3.7, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- battery_lifetime: {value: 18, unit_symbol: \"years\"}\n- display_digits: {value: 9}? From description: \"electronic scrolling LCD with 9 digits (range 999999,999 m³)\". Calculator capacity covers range. I'll add display_digits: 9 in type_level, and calculator capacity in config_level.\n- measurement_principle: {value: \"ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time\"}\n\nmodel_level:\n- nominal_diameter_dn: values DN15/DN20/DN25 (string values \"DN15\" etc., unit mm)\n- permanent_flowrate_q3: 2.5, 4, 6.3 m³/h\n- overload_flowrate_q4: 3.125, 5, 7.875 m³/h\n- connection_thread: \"G ¾ B\", \"G1 B\", \"G1 ¼ B\" (unit mm per table, but thread designations — I'll keep unit null or \"mm\"? The table says unit mm for connection thread. Value strings \"G ¾ B\". I'll set unit_symbol null since it's a designation; hmm table says mm. I'll keep unit_symbol \"mm\"? That seems wrong for a thread designation. I'll use null.)\n\nHmm, \"G ¾ B\" — should I normalize to \"G 3/4 B\"? The markdown has ¾ Unicode fraction. I'll keep \"G 3/4 B\"? Rule: no fabrication; ¾ → \"3/4\" is a faithful conversion. I'll write \"G 3/4 B\" to avoid Unicode fraction issues? Actually JSON handles ¾ fine. I'll keep \"G ¾ B\" as-is. Either is fine. I'll keep original.\n\n- construction_length_l: values \"110/115/145/165\" etc. as strings with unit mm. Or lists? Value must be number/range/string. String \"110 / 115 / 145 / 165\" with unit mm. OK.\n\nHmm wait, maybe I should double check the DN20 construction lengths: \"130/165/190/195/220\" — 5 values. Yes as given.\n\nconfig_level:\n1. minimum_flowrate_q1 — axis \"nominal diameter and meter ratio R (Q3/Q1)\":\n   values:\n   - {condition: \"MA408 (DN15), R160\", value: 0.015625, unit m³/h}\n   - {condition: \"MA408 (DN15), R250\", value: 0.01}\n   - {condition: \"MA408 (DN15), R400\", value: 0.00625}\n   - {condition: \"MA408 (DN15), R500\", value: 0.005}\n   - {condition: \"MA408 (DN20), R160\", value: 0.025}\n   - {condition: \"MA408 (DN20), R250\", value: 0.016}\n   - {condition: \"MA408 (DN20), R400\", value: 0.01}\n   - {condition: \"MA408 (DN20), R500\", value: 0.008}\n   - {condition: \"MA408 (DN25), R160\", value: 0.039375}\n   - {condition: \"MA408 (DN25), R250\", value: 0.0252}\n   - {condition: \"MA408 (DN25), R400\", value: 0.01575}\n   - {condition: \"MA408 (DN25), R500\", value: 0.0126}\n\n   Wait — but the table lists Q1 values in order \"0.015625 / 0.01 / 0.00625 / 0.005\" — is the order R160, R250, R400, R500? For DN15 Q3=2.5: 2.5/160=0.015625 ✓, 2.5/250=0.01 ✓, 2.5/400=0.00625 ✓, 2.5/500=0.005 ✓. And the ratio row says \"R160, R250, R400, R500 (all sizes)\". So yes the order matches. This is a reasonable inference, mapping the slash-list to the listed ratios in order. I'm confident in this mapping since it's mathematically consistent (Q1 = Q3/R exactly for all 12). Good.\n\n   Similarly Q2 = 1.6 × Q1:\n   DN15: 0.025, 0.016, 0.01, 0.008 ✓ matches.\n   DN20: 0.04, 0.0256, 0.016, 0.0128 ✓.\n   DN25: 0.063, 0.04032, 0.0252, 0.02016 ✓.\n\n2. transitional_flowrate_q2 — same axis, 12 values as above.\n\n3. maximum_permissible_error — axis \"flow zone and water temperature\":\n   - {condition: \"lower flow rates (Q1 ≤ Q < Q2)\", value: 5, unit \"%\"}\n   - {condition: \"upper flow rates (Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4), water temperature ≤ 30 °C\", value: 2, unit \"%\"}\n   - {condition: \"upper flow rates (Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4), water temperature > 30 °C\", value: 3, unit \"%\"}\n   \n   Note ± signs. Should I note the ±? The attribute name \"maximum_permissible_error\" with ± implied. I'll add \"(±)\" to attribute? Keep attribute \"maximum_permissible_error_mpe\", values positive magnitudes; the ± is implied by MPE definition. Fine.\n\n4. calculator_capacity — axis \"display mode\":\n   - {condition: \"normal mode\", value: 999999.999, unit \"m³\"}\n   - {condition: \"testing mode\", value: 999.999999, unit \"m³\"}\n\n5. scale_interval — axis \"resolution mode\":\n   - {condition: \"normal resolution\", value: 0.001, unit \"m³\"}\n   - {condition: \"high resolution\", value: 0.000001, unit \"m³\"}\n\n6. ratio_q3_q1 — axis \"available meter ratio (all models)\": conditions \"all models\" values R160 etc. Or type_level string. I'll put in config_level as axis \"meter ratio option\", values [{condition: \"MA408 (all sizes)\", value: \"R160\"}, ...]. Hmm. Alternatively type_level: {\"ratio_q3_q1_available_values\": {\"value\": \"R160, R250, R400, R500\"}}. I think config_level is cleaner:\n\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"ratio_q3_q1\",\n  \"axis\": \"meter ratio option\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"all models\", \"value\": \"R160\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"all models\", \"value\": \"R250\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"all models\", \"value\": \"R400\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"all models\", \"value\": \"R500\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nOK.\n\nmatrix_tables: Should I include the page 4 table as a matrix_table too? The model_level + config_level capture it. The R137 example includes both matrix_tables AND model_level. So include the page-4 table as a matrix table (transposed to rows = models? or keep as given rows = characteristics?). The example format: columns[], rows[] (object per row). For the transposed table, I'll keep original orientation: rows are characteristics. Let me define:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"Technical and metrological data\",\n  \"columns\": [\"characteristic\", \"unit\", \"MA408 (DN15)\", \"MA408 (DN20)\", \"MA408 (DN25)\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Nominal diameter DN\", \"unit\": \"mm\", \"MA408 (DN15)\": \"DN15\", \"MA408 (DN20)\": \"DN20\", \"MA408 (DN25)\": \"DN25\"},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Permanent flowrate Q3\", \"unit\": \"m³/h\", \"MA408 (DN15)\": 2.5, \"MA408 (DN20)\": 4, \"MA408 (DN25)\": 6.3},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Minimum flowrate Q1\", \"unit\": \"m³/h\", \"MA408 (DN15)\": \"0.015625 / 0.01 / 0.00625 / 0.005\", ...},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Transitional flowrate Q2\", \"unit\": \"m³/h\", ...},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Overload flowrate Q4\", \"unit\": \"m³/h\", 3.125, 5, 7.875},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Ratio Q3/Q1\", \"unit\": \"R\", \"all sizes\": \"R160, R250, R400, R500\"},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Ratio Q2/Q1\", \"unit\": null, 1.6},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Connection thread\", \"unit\": \"mm\", \"G ¾ B\", \"G1 B\", \"G1 ¼ B\"},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Construction length L\", \"unit\": \"mm\", \"110/115/145/165\", \"130/165/190/195/220\", \"175/200/225/260\"},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Installation orientation\", \"unit\": null, \"H/V\"},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Water temperature range (temperature class)\", \"unit\": \"°C\", \"0.1 to 50 (T50)\"}\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"Q1 and Q2 list values in order of meter ratio R160, R250, R400, R500.\",\n  \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\nHmm, rows objects with varying keys — row for ratio rows would have model keys missing. Could fill null. Let me structure rows with all model keys, using a \"scope\" maybe. Simpler: keep per-row keys only as needed. JSON objects per row are free-form. I'll fill null for non-applicable model cells and put shared values in all three columns? E.g., ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6 for all — put 1.6 in each column? That's an interpretation but true. The table shows merged cells. For \"Ratio Q3/Q1\" the cell spans: \"R160, R250, R400, R500 (all sizes)\". I'll put the string in each column with the note. Or use null. I'll fill values in all three columns where the characteristic applies to all, since that's accurate.\n\nActually let me keep it simple and faithful: rows with keys \"characteristic\", \"unit\", and the three model columns; shared values repeated across columns; for water temperature range \"0.1 to 50 (T50)\" repeated.\n\nThat duplicates but is fine.\n\nAlternatively skip matrix_tables since model_level+config_level capture everything. But rule 4 R137 example says extract as matrix_tables AND populate model_level. I'll include the matrix table.\n\nAlso page 5 table (Parameter | Unit | Value) — that's type-level specs, captured in type_level. No need for matrix table.\n\nDate formats: \"10 September 2026\" → \"2026-09-10\". \"9 September 2026\" → \"2026-09-09\".\n\ncertificate.page_total: 7.\n\nCategory: \"Water meter\".\n\nLet me also double-check certificate number: \"R49/2024-A-SK1-26.01\". Include as-is.\n\nProject number: not present. Omit.\n\noiml_issuer_id: not present.\n\nNow software spec (page 3 section 2.3): software version V000101, checksum C93395DB. This is legally relevant software — where to put? Not in schema explicitly. Could go in characteristics.type_level: legal_software_version: {value: \"V000101\"}, software_checksum: {value: \"C93395DB\"}. Yes, I'll add those.\n\nBattery: page 3 says \"one non-replaceable lithium battery (lifetime 18 years)\" — consistent with page 5.\n\nDisplay: 9 digits LCD, range 999999,999 m³ → calculator capacity normal mode. Good.\n\nAccuracy class 2 also appears in page 5 table. Included.\n\nAdditional type_level items: \n- measurement_principle\n- display_type: \"electronic scrolling LCD\"? Could add. Keep description mention.\n- installation_positions: covered by installation_orientation \"H/V\".\n- reverse_flow: \"not designed to measure reverse flow\" — add type_level: reverse_flow_measurement: {value: false}? or \"not measured\". I'll add \"measures_reverse_flow\": false? Hmm, could be fabrication-ish but it's stated. I'll include in description instead to be safe. Actually a boolean from explicit statement is fine: \"reverse_flow\": {value: \"not designed to measure\"}. I'll just leave in description.\n\nNow, unit for battery lifetime: \"years\". unit_id null.\n\nQ3 DN20 value: \"4\" → 4. DN25 \"6,3\" → 6.3. DN15 \"2,5\" → 2.5. Q4: \"3,125\" → 3.125, 5, \"7,875\" → 7.875.\n\nQ2/Q1 ratio 1,6 → 1.6.\n\nClimatic class −25 to +55 °C → {min: -25, max: 55}.\n\nMAP 16 bar.\n\nPressure loss \"0,40 / Δp 40\": maximum_pressure_loss 0.4 bar; pressure_loss_class \"Δp40\". \n\nMPE: values ±2, ±3, ±5 → magnitudes 2, 3, 5.\n\nLet me now also consider model label vs id: I'll use model_id \"MA408 DN15\" and label \"MA408 (DN15)\". Or keep identical. I'll set model_id \"MA408 (DN15)\" to match the table headers exactly, label same. For consistency with config conditions, use \"MA408 (DN15)\".\n\nmodel attributes: {nominal_dn: \"DN15\"}. Actually attributes{} per model — put nominal_diameter: \"DN15\". Fine.\n\nNow assemble the JSON. Ensure valid JSON: no trailing commas, proper escaping of m³ (Unicode fine), °C fine, ≤ fine, Δ fine.\n\nLet me write the final JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2024-A-SK1-26.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"page_total\": 7,\n    \"member_state\": \"Slovakia\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2026-09-10\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\n      \"Geologická 9966/1\",\n      \"821 06 Bratislava-Podunajské Biskupice\",\n      \"Slovakia\"\n    ],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"Ing. Dušan Šmigura, PhD.\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Director of PCB\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Shenzhen Kaifa Technology (Chengdu) Co., Ltd.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\n        \"No. 99 Tianquan Road\",\n        \"Hi-Tech Development Zone\",\n        \"Chengdu, Sichuan\",\n        \"P.R. China\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Shenzhen Kaifa Technology (Chengdu) Co., Ltd.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\n        \"No. 99 Tianquan Road\",\n        \"Hi-Tech Development Zone\",\n        \"Chengdu, Sichuan\",\n        \"P.R. China\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MA408\"],\n    \"module_designation\": \"Ultrasonic water meters with electronic indication device\",\n    \"description\": \"Residential compact ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device that measures, memorizes and displays the volume of water passing through the measurement transducers at metering conditions; intended for clean water in residential use. Based on the ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time principle. Can be installed in horizontal and vertical positions; not designed to measure reverse flow. Non-essential parts: strainer, optical communication port, one communication battery.\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"MA408\",\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"MA408 (DN15)\", \"label\": \"MA408 (DN15)\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter\": \"DN15\"}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"MA408 (DN20)\", \"label\": \"MA408 (DN20)\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter\": \"DN20\"}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"MA408 (DN25)\", \"label\": \"MA408 (DN25)\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter\": \"DN25\"}}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [\n    {\n      \"role\": \"flow sensor (essential part)\",\n      \"type_designations\": [],\n      \"characteristics\": {\n        \"description\": {\"value\": \"Brass cylindrical body with inlet/outlet, inner plastic measuring supports (pipe support-down/up), two reflection sheets in the pipe centre, and two ultrasonic transducers (upstream and downstream).\"},\n        \"measurement_principle\": {\"value\": \"ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time\"}\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"role\": \"calculator and indication device (essential part)\",\n      \"type_designations\": [],\n      \"characteristics\": {\n        \"description\": {\"value\": \"Plastic housing mounted directly on the flow sensor; one PCB (main + measurement board combined); electronic scrolling LCD with 9 digits (range 999999.999 m³); one non-replaceable lithium battery (lifetime 18 years).\"}\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"measurement_principle\": {\"value\": \"ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"water_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"temperature_class\": {\"value\": \"T50\", ...},\n      \"installation_orientation\": {\"value\": \"H/V\", ...},\n      \"maximum_admissible_pressure_map\": {\"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n      \"maximum_pressure_loss\": {\"value\": 0.4, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n      \"pressure_loss_class\": {\"value\": \"Δp40\"},\n      \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": 2},\n      \"mechanical_environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"M1\"},\n      \"climatic_class\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n      \"electromagnetic_environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"E1/E2\"},\n      \"environmental_class_r49_1_climatic_mechanical\": {\"value\": \"B/O\"},\n      \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\": {\"value\": \"U0D0\"},\n      \"battery_type\": {\"value\": \"non-replaceable lithium battery\"},\n      \"battery_maximum_voltage\": {\"value\": 3.7, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n      \"battery_lifetime\": {\"value\": 18, \"unit_symbol\": \"years\"},\n      \"display_digit_count\": {\"value\": 9},\n      \"legal_software_version\": {\"value\": \"V000101\"},\n      \"legal_software_checksum\": {\"value\": \"C93395DB\"}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"nominal_diameter\", \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\", \"values\": [\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN15)\", \"value\": \"DN15\"},\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN20)\", \"value\": \"DN20\"},\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN25)\", \"value\": \"DN25\"}\n      ]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN15)\", \"value\": 2.5},\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN20)\", \"value\": 4},\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN25)\", \"value\": 6.3}\n      ]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN15)\", \"value\": 3.125},\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN20)\", \"value\": 5},\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN25)\", \"value\": 7.875}\n      ]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"connection_thread\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN15)\", \"value\": \"G ¾ B\"},\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN20)\", \"value\": \"G1 B\"},\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN25)\", \"value\": \"G1 ¼ B\"}\n      ]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"construction_length\", \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\", \"values\": [\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN15)\", \"value\": \"110 / 115 / 145 / 165\"},\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN20)\", \"value\": \"130 / 165 / 190 / 195 / 220\"},\n        {\"model\": \"MA408 (DN25)\", \"value\": \"175 / 200 / 225 / 260\"}\n      ]}\n    ],\n    \"config_level\": [\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"minimum_flowrate_q1\",\n        \"axis\": \"nominal diameter × meter ratio R (Q3/Q1)\",\n        \"values\": [\n          {\"condition\": \"MA408 (DN15), R160\", \"value\": 0.015625, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"},\n          {\"condition\": \"MA408 (DN15), R250\", \"value\": 0.01, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"},\n          {\"condition\": \"MA408 (DN15), R400\", \"value\": 0.006