ix_tables\": [...],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2024,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [\"2\"]\n  },\n  \"test_reports\": [\n    {\"id\": \"2026/ER040/SK1\", \"date\": \"2026-09-10\", \"pages\": 29, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n    {\"id\": \"Shenzhen Kaifa_MA408_00_OIML R49:2024\", \"date\": \"2026-09-09\", \"pages\": 118, \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n  ],\n  \"revision_history\": [\n    {\"revision\": \"0\", \"date\": \"2026-09-10\", \"changes\": \"Certificate first issued\"}\n  ],\n  \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\nFor footnotes: none defined → omit or empty array? Schema includes footnotes[]; I'll omit it (rule 8: omit if not present). Or empty. I'll omit.\n\nThe description says \"unit mm\" for connection thread — I set unit_symbol null since \"G ¾ B\" is a designation, not a length. Fine.\n\nAlso should the ratio_q2_q1 be unit \"–\"? dimensionless, unit null.\n\nNow the matrix_tables entry. Let me write it:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"Technical and metrological data\",\n  \"columns\": [\"characteristic\", \"unit\", \"ma408_dn15\", \"ma408_dn20\", \"ma408_dn25\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Nominal diameter DN\", \"unit\": \"mm\", \"ma408_dn15\": \"DN15\", \"ma408_dn20\": \"DN20\", \"ma408_dn25\": \"DN25\"},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Permanent flowrate Q3\", \"unit\": \"m³/h\", \"ma408_dn15\": 2.5, \"ma408_dn20\": 4, \"ma408_dn25\": 6.3},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Minimum flowrate Q1\", \"unit\": \"m³/h\", \"ma408_dn15\": \"0.015625 / 0.01 / 0.00625 / 0.005\", \"ma408_dn20\": \"0.025 / 0.016 / 0.01 / 0.008\", \"ma408_dn25\": \"0.039375 / 0.0252 / 0.01575 / 0.0126\"},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Transitional flowrate Q2\", \"unit\": \"m³/h\", \"ma408_dn15\": \"0.025 / 0.016 / 0.01 / 0.008\", \"ma408_dn20\": \"0.04 / 0.0256 / 0.016 / 0.0128\", \"ma408_dn25\": \"0.063 / 0.04032 / 0.0252 / 0.02016\"},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Overload flowrate Q4\", \"unit\": \"m³/h\", \"ma408_dn15\": 3.125, \"ma408_dn20\": 5, \"ma408_dn25\": 7.875},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Ratio Q3/Q1\", \"unit\": \"R\", \"ma408_dn15\": \"R160 / R250 / R400 / R500\", \"ma408_dn20\": \"R160 / R250 / R400 / R500\", \"ma408_dn25\": \"R160 / R250 / R400 / R500\"},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Ratio Q2/Q1\", \"unit\": null, \"ma408_dn15\": 1.6, \"ma408_dn20\": 1.6, \"ma408_dn25\": 1.6},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Connection thread\", \"unit\": \"mm\", \"ma408_dn15\": \"G ¾ B\", \"ma408_dn20\": \"G1 B\", \"ma408_dn25\": \"G1 ¼ B\"},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Construction length L\", \"unit\": \"mm\", \"ma408_dn15\": \"110 / 115 / 145 / 165\", \"ma408_dn20\": \"130 / 165 / 190 / 195 / 220\", \"ma408_dn25\": \"175 / 200 / 225 / 260\"},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Installation orientation\", \"unit\": null, \"ma408_dn15\": \"H/V\", \"ma408_dn20\": \"H/V\", \"ma408_dn25\": \"H/V\"},\n    {\"characteristic\": \"Water temperature range (temperature class)\", \"unit\": \"°C\", \"ma408_dn15\": \"0.1 to 50 (T50)\", \"ma408_dn20\": \"0.1 to 50 (T50)\", \"ma408_dn25\": \"0.1 to 50 (T50)\"}\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"Q1 and Q2 values are listed in the order of meter ratio R160, R250, R400, R500 (Q1 = Q3/R, Q2 = 1.6 × Q1); construction lengths are the available options per nominal diameter.\",\n  \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\nDecimal conversions: page-4 table used \"2,5\" → 2.5 etc. In the matrix table I should use converted decimals (rule: decimal point). Yes, done.\n\nThe temperature \"0,1 to 50\" → \"0.1 to 50 (T50)\". OK.\n\nCheck type_level keys canonical: \n- \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" (MAP is in the label). I'll use \"maximum_admissible_pressure_map\".\n- \"pressure_loss_class\": \"Δp40\" — markdown says \"Δp 40\". I'll use \"Δp40\". Hmm original \"Δp 40\"; keep \"Δp 40\"? I'll write \"Δp40\" — minor normalization; fine either way. Use \"Δp 40\"? I'll use \"Δp 40\" to be faithful. Eh, \"Δp40\" is the standard class designation. Keep \"Δp 40\" faithful... I'll go with \"Δp 40\".\n\nActually, let me reconsider \"environmental_class_r49_1_climatic_mechanical\": The label \"Climatic/mechanical environmental class (OIML R 49-1)\" with value \"B/O\". Canonical label: \"climatic_mechanical_environmental_class_r49_1\"? Snake case, no parentheses. I'll use \"climatic_mechanical_environmental_class_oiml_r49_1\". Hmm simpler: \"environmental_class_climatic_mechanical\". The B/O = climatic B / mechanical O. I'll use \"climatic_mechanical_environmental_class\" value \"B/O\".\n\nWait — but mechanical class M1 and climatic class −25 to +55 also present. Those are separate rows: \"Mechanical class M1\", \"Climatic class −25 to +55 °C\", \"Electromagnetic class E1/E2\", \"Climatic/mechanical environmental class (OIML R 49-1): B/O\". OK keep all four.\n\n\"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\": \"U0D0\" — original \"U0D0\". Good.\n\nType-level battery lifetime: 18 years — appears in both page 3 and page 5. One entry.\n\ndisplay_digit_count 9 — from description \"electronic scrolling LCD with 9 digits\". Add.\n\nAlso \"software version and checksum can be checked using the scrolling display\" — no need.\n\nAccountable alarms: \"If a fault condition occurs and the measurement stops, follow the user manual\" — not a characteristic. Could go in description; skip or add to certified_type.description? I'll leave out; not critical. Hmm, maybe include briefly? I'll skip — description is already long. Actually it's fine to omit.\n\nDo I need \"project_number\"? Not present. Omit.\n\n\"oiml_issuer_id\": omit.\n\nrecommendation.accuracy_classes: [\"2\"] — schema says accuracy_classes[] — for water meter class 2. Use [\"2\"]? Or [2]? R60 example in prompt: \"accuracy_classes[]\" not exemplified. Certificates typically list \"2\". I'll use [\"2\"].\n\nScheme in certificate: \"A\" — from \"Scheme A\". Good.\n\nNow — value ±2 for MPE: I'm recording 2 and 3 and 5 without sign. To preserve ±, I could set attribute \"maximum_permissible_error_mpe\" and value \"±2\"? Rule says numbers not